#the .gitignore file is your primary defense against accidentally sharing sensitive information
# (like API keys or internal project IDs) with the world.
#Prevents uploading "junk" files that Python creates when it runs.
# --- Python Specifics ---
__pycache__/
venv/
.env

# --- GCP & Security (Critical for CloudSec SRE) ---
# Prevents leaking service account keys or private JSON configs [cite: 17]
*.json
.gcloud/

# --- Infrastructure as Code (Terraform) ---
# Prevents uploading local state which contains sensitive metadata [cite: 11, 18]
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.backup

# --- System Logs & Junk ---
*.log
.DS_Store

#Professionalism: Recruiters looking at your GitHub will see that you understand Security Best Practices,
#which is vital for a CloudSec SRE role.

#Cleaner Commits: It ensures your "1 year of experience" looks organized rather than cluttered with 
#temporary system files.
#Safety: As you transition from a Solutions Engineer to an SRE, 
#protecting infrastructure access (IAM) is a core part of your responsibility.


